<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.11.5: Using Relationship Models to Answer Questions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01450</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.11.5: Using Relationship Models to Answer Questions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e81828f6-2f20-4e74-85c8-ae1d7e54cbaf</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para id="para-00001">A ball is dropped from a certain height. The table shows the rebound heights of the ball after a series of bounces.
</para>
<table class="unnumbered os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00001">
  <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>Bounce Number</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Height in Centimeters</emphasis></entry></row>
  <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        30
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        6
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>

<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    
    <para id="para-00002">From what height, approximately, do you think the ball was dropped? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00003">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
  <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00005">Between 150 cm and 180 cm. The rebound factors are <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>, and 0 (this last measurement is
    probably not reliable because it could have been a very small bounce height, which would be difficult to measure).
    Because <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of 150 is 30 and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of 180 is 30, the ball was probably dropped from between 150 cm and
    180 cm.</para>

</solution></exercise>
</section>
</content></document>
